  rose up shortly afterwards but they were  less interested in concessions are more  interested in kicking the planted  English Protestants out of Ireland for  good  back in England Parliament was still  fighting over how much King Charles was  awful and in late 1641 by a very slim  margin it passed the grand remonstrance  this was basically a list of reasons why  Charles was terrible and that the  previous Church reforms of Lord in the  Irish rebellion were part of a Catholic  plot by 1642 Charles had had enough of  people opposing him and decided to put  an end to it he gathered some soldiers  are marched into Parliament with the  intent of arresting five of its members  the most notable being John Pym these  members had fled before Charles had  arrived and he left Parliament  empty-handed  whilst he failed to arrest those who  deposed him he did manage to do one  thing he convinced everyone that he was  a tyrant as a result Parliament created  the Committee of Safety which promptly  took control of London as well as its  militia and Charles quickly fled the  city both sides started to call  Eliezer made preparations for conflicts  such as when Charles attempted to seize  HoloNet armory but they politely yet  firmly told him to jog on Parliament  declared that Charles was a papist and  wanted to enslave England with the help  of the Irish rebels and Charles warned  the aristocracy the Parliament would  come for them next if the radicals had  their way on the 22nd of August 1642  Charles raised his standard over the  town of Nottingham this is considered by  many to be the formal beginning of the  English Civil War this pitted Charles's  royalist forces known as Cavaliers  against those of Parliament known as the  round heads as the general rule  Parliament had the support of the south  and the east and Charles had the rest  Parliament had much greater access to  money from the largest cities in the  south it also controlled the Navy and  with it trade the king had some support  in Ireland the help of the Scottish  Royalists and had access to much better  commanders the most notable being Prince  Rupert of the Rhine Charles's nephew  from Bohemia Charles assembled his  forces and made straight for London  where he was met by the parliamentary  army led by Robert Devereux the Earl of  Essex at the Battle of Edgehill it was  essentially a tie and after regrouping  Charles did the done what to do next  before resuming his march on London by  this point the Earl of Essex had blocked  the road there and Charles retreated  meaning that this wasn't going to be a  short war throughout 1643 the royalist  forces gained the upper hand the  Parliamentarians did manage to procure  the help of the Scottish Covenanter army  though by promising them lots of cash  the Scottish also wanted assurances that  the Church of England would be modeled  after the Church of Scotland naturally  Parliament was never going to agree to  this and so his leaders hatched a clever  plan they lied the Covenant is were  vital to the parliamentarian cause and  proved their worth in 1644 at the Battle  of Marston Moor it was here that the  royalist army was devastated  particularly by the parliamentarian  cavalry led by a certain Oliver Cromwell  after this defeat the Royalists  basically lost the entire north and York  fell shortly afterwards despite their  success Parliament wanted more and so  ordered the far-reaching reforms be  implemented which led to the creation of  the professional well-equipped new model  army its importance of know here that  not all of the country was happy to be  involved in the Civil War many local  Lords and leaders wanted nothing to do  with it and so assemble with their own  soldiers known as club men to dissuade  either side from coming anywhere near  them 1645 saw the decisive battle of  Naseby which was yet again a crushing  defeat for the Royalists and was  followed up by many more over the next  year  Charles knowing that the war was lost  handed himself over to a Scottish force  in mid 1646 ending the Civil War  Parliament negotiate  with the Scottish to hand over Charles  which they did in 1647 now the  Parliament had chose surely they could  enact the change they wanted right but  no the issue was that Charles was  completely untrustworthy and would  always take the first opportunity to  undo the concessions he'd made  furthermore much of England was getting  riotous due to the tax burden for  funding the army Parliament tried to  disband the army but the army led by Sir  Thomas Fairfax said no which was hard to  argue with because guns this reflects  the fact that political society in  England was becoming increasingly  radical during this period one of the  most extreme groups were the Levellers  who wanted the army to through our  Parliament and create a democratic ie  level society in which all Freeborn men  would be able to vote for accountable  politicians during his captivity Charles  had secretly made a compact with the  Scottish called the engagement which  meant that they would invade England and  restore him with his old powers intact  Charles soon escaped the Scottish  invaded and royalist rebellions broke  out across the country beginning the  second English Civil War this war was a  short one and within a year the New  Model Army had crushed the rebels and  defeated the Scottish army at the Battle  of Preston Charles decided to return to  the negotiating table but the leadership  of the New Model Army known as the  Grandy's weren't interested they saw  Charles as a lying tyrant whose actions  had killed thousands needlessly and that  there would be no worthwhile settlement  whilst he remained in late 1648 some  soldiers commanded by Colonel Thomas  pride stormed Parliament and removed  anyone who disagreed with the Grandy's  in what is known as prides purge the  parliament that was left known as the  rump Parliament opted to put Charles on  trial for high treason he was found  guilty sentenced to death and on the  30th of January 1649 was beheaded at  Whitehall palace shortly afterwards the  Republic was declared called the  Commonwealth of England which was  governed by the rump Parliament in the  Council of State chaired by Cromwell  Parliament and the council didn't really  do much except sell-off royal assets and  lands in order to fund the army to send  it to Ireland the Irish rebels known as  the Irish Confederates were being  supported by English and Scottish  Royalists who had rallied behind Charles  his son also called Charles Cromer was  tasked with the reconquest of Ireland  and with his army of well acquit  veterans made inroads very quickly  he gave the Irish assurances that those  who surrendered would not be harmed but  when he took the towns of Drogheda and  Wexford he promptly slaughtered their  Garrison's and many of the civilians  living there  Cromwell returned to England in 1650 but  the conquest was largely completed in  his absence by 1652  Ireland was completely ransacked during  this invasion and it's estimated that  about 40% of its total population died  between  16:41 and 1652 it was then ordered that  the rebels were to lose their lands and  about 35,000 of them were forced into  indentured servitude and shipped off to  the new world to work on the plantations  they're speaking of the new world most  of the American colonies backed the  royalist side but with great to support  for Cromwell in the north so upon his  return to England Cromwell had to deal  with the Scottish who had received  Charles and proclaimed him as charles ii  of scotland beginning the third english  civil war chromo marched his army north  and crushed a larger force at the Battle  of Dunbar and swiftly occupied Edinburgh  the next year the two met again at  Worcester and yet again Charles lost and  promptly ran away to the continent  ending once and furro the English Civil  Wars by 1653 Cromwell was getting a bit  sick of the rump Parliament and so  dismissed most of its members he then  created a parliament made up of a select  few men who were considered to be deeply  moral in what is known as the Parliament  of the saints or the bare-bones  Parliament this Parliament failed to do  anything and so Cromwell had a  constitution craft it called the  instrument of government which declared  Cromwell to be the Lord Protector of the  Commonwealth of England Scotland and  Ireland that is to say that all three  were now part of the same state meaning  Cromwell with the help of the Scottish  General George Monck could achieve what  all the previous kings of England had  failed to anyway cromwell's rule was  marred with financial problems and it  also saw the Commonwealth dragged into  numerous Wars the first was the first  anglo-dutch war in 1652 which started  because of the 1651 navigation Act which  prevented English colonies in North  America from trading with foreign States  long story short the Dutch lost but very  little changed this wasn't to be  Cromwell's last foreign war since in  1654 war broke out with Spain in the end  the Commonwealth seized Jamaica in the  Cayman Islands in the Caribbean as well  as Dunkirk in Europe  back in England Cromwell was struggling  with his new regime in 1657 after a  failed period of direct military rule a  new constitution was passed and Cromwell  was offered the crown which he declined  resistance to his rule was starting to  grow and so he had to resort to much  fiercer tactics to get what he wanted  this included taxing without the consent  of parliament and throwing those who  disagreed with him in prison without  trial of course he could do nothing  about those who disagreed with him  outside of England such as Thomas Hobbes  who whilst living in France wrote his  magnum opus Leviathan this was a defence  of absolute monarchy like that in France  and condemned the concept of a republic  chromos troubles came to an end in 1658  because he died the title of Lord  Protector passed to his son Richard  Cromwell because again it was nothing  like a monarchy basically no-one wanted  Richard  - rollin in 1659 he resigned in the rump  Parliament returned to run the country  thistle went to hell pretty quickly and  it looked like another civil war was  brewing so general monk marched down  south once in London he reinstated all  of the members of parliament who had  been ousted in prides purge in 1660  Parliament the same one that had been in  session since 1642 dissolved itself  hence white is known as the Long  Parliament during this time monk had  been in contact with Charles who issued  what was called the Declaration of Breda  for giving everything that happens in  1649 so long as the people there  accepted him as their king the exception  to this forgiveness was of course those  who signed his father's death warrant  the new parliament was summoned called  the convention Parliament which  acknowledged towers as Charles ii of  England and their rightful king in May  of 1660 Charles returned ending the  Commonwealth and England's Republican  experiment I hope you enjoyed this  episode and thank you for watching and a  special thanks to James Bissonnette sol  invictus and dante of Livonia for  supporting the channel if you'd like to  know more about the English Civil War in  the Commonwealth period then there are  some book recommendations in the  description below

